@@s001 Code: pyramid (Tower) in Java
@@s002 Code: pyramid (Tower) in Java
@@s003 DP from the bottom up on a pyramid: arrays of rows with growing length, and the maximum of the two children.
@@s004 Reading the pyramid
@@s005 Each level <code>r</code> has <code>r + 1</code> values: an array of variable length for each row.
@@s006 Reading the pyramid
@@s007 An array of arrays with no fixed length: <code>new int[n][]</code> creates the rows, then each one is sized.
@@s008 Row r has r + 1 elements.
@@s009 Syntax notes
@@s010 <code>new int[n][]</code> leaves the rows empty: they are sized afterwards. It is the way to have jagged arrays.
@@s011 Climbing the pyramid
@@s012 The last row is the base case. Each row above takes its own value plus the maximum of the two children.
@@s013 Climbing the pyramid
@@s014 Base case: the last row. <code>clone()</code> copies the array, so I do not modify the pyramid.
@@s015 Recurrence: value of the cell plus the maximum of the two children below.
@@s016 Syntax notes
@@s017 Only two rows are needed in memory at any time: the new row replaces the old one.
